auto-inc-dec: turn chains of reg+disp accesses into post-increments
and pre-decrements [PR50749]

auto-inc-dec pairs one memory access with one increment.  Loops that
read several consecutive values per iteration reach it as

    *(r + 0); *(r + 4); *(r + 8); r = r + 12;

and none of the accesses pairs with the add, so SH2 kept displacement
addressing plus the add (and byte/word displacement loads tie up r0):

    before:  mov.l @r4,r1 / mov.l @(4,r4),r2 / mov.l @(8,r4),r0 / add #12,r4
    after:   mov.l @r4+,r1 / mov.l @r4+,r2 / mov.l @r4+,r0

New merge_inc_chain runs before the existing matching: for "a = r + C"
it walks back from the add; if the accesses before it are MEMs at
offsets 0, s1, s1+s2, ... (s = access size) adding up to exactly C, it
rewrites them all as (post_inc r) in one validated change group and
deletes the add.  For C < 0 it matches stores at C, C + s, ..., -s and
makes them (pre_dec r): "*--p = x; *--p = y; return p;".  Anything
else using r in that range (a call, asm, a non-MEM use, a second use in
one insn, a REG_EQUAL note) stops it.  The target validates each form
(SH has no post-increment stores and no pre-decrement loads).  Debug
insns in the range are rebased.

Two more shapes are common and handled:

- the add writes another register a, typically the returned pointer
  ("x = *p++; y = *p++; return p;"): a = r is inserted before the chain
  and a is incremented instead, so r and its other uses are untouched
  (a must not appear between the chain and the add):

    before:  mov.l @r4,r1 / mov r4,r0 / mov.l @(4,r4),r2 / add #8,r0
    after:   mov r4,r0 / mov.l @r0+,r1 / mov.l @r0+,r2

- the accesses go through a pseudo set to r + k, which expand creates
  on SH for the negative displacements of "*--p" stores:

    before:  mov r4,r1 / add #-64,r1 / mov r4,r0 / mov.l r5,@(60,r1)
             add #-8,r0 / mov.l r5,@(56,r1)
    after:   mov r4,r0 / mov.l r5,@-r0 / mov.l r5,@-r0

Not covered: a loop that stores backwards several times per iteration
(pr50749-qihisi-predec-4.c).  ivopts gives it a base 3 accesses lower,
so the stores are at +8, +4, +0 before "r -= 12"; making them
pre-decrements needs the induction variable rebased, which is ivopts'
job.

Tested on sh-elf -m2 (GCC 14.2.0, post-increment chains only;
auto-inc-dec.cc is identical in 14.3.0): gcc.c-torture/execute on the
GDB SH simulator at -O1, -O2, -O3, -Os, -O2 -funroll-loops,
-Os -funroll-all-loops and -O3 -g: no result changed (1374 passes per
option set before and after).  gcc.c-torture compile+execute at
-O2 -g -fcompare-debug: no ICEs, no compare-debug failures.  .text:
-O2 -0.001%, -Os -0.002%, -O2 -funroll-loops -0.016%, no file grew.

